---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="cm-to-inches">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="cm-to-inches" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Centimetres to inches, exactly</h2>
    <p>
      The inch has been a metric unit since 1959. In that year the United States, the United Kingdom,
      Canada, Australia, New Zealand and South Africa signed the international yard and pound
      agreement, which fixed the inch at <strong>exactly 2.54 centimetres</strong>. This is a
      definition, not a measured ratio, so each conversion on this page is exact to as many decimal
      places as you ask for.
    </p>
    <p>There are two ways to write the same arithmetic:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Divide by 2.54: 30 cm &divide; 2.54 = 11.811 in.</li>
      <li>Multiply by 0.3937007874: 30 &times; 0.3937007874 = 11.811 in.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      By hand, division is safer: 2.54 is short and easy to remember, and the reciprocal is a recurring
      decimal that you must truncate. This page divides with arbitrary-precision decimal arithmetic, not
      the binary floating point of the browser, so you do not see results such as 11.810999999999998.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the fraction column matters</h2>
    <p>
      Imperial tape measures show sixteenths, not decimal inches. A result of 11.811 in is not useful at
      the bench until you know it is just under 11 and thirteen-sixteenths, which the fraction readout
      shows. The three common resolutions are:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Resolution</th><th scope="col">Step size</th><th scope="col">Used for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1/16 in</td><td>1.5875 mm</td><td>Builder's tape, general carpentry, fabric</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1/32 in</td><td>0.7938 mm</td><td>Cabinetmaking, sheet goods</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1/64 in</td><td>0.3969 mm</td><td>Machining, engraving, model making</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The converter rounds to sixteenths by default, because most tapes show them. The
      millimetres-to-inches page shows all three side by side for finer work.
    </p>

    <h2>Common centimetre measurements in inches</h2>
    <p>
      Some conversions are worth knowing by heart. A 30 cm ruler is 11.81 in, just under a foot. A4
      paper is 21 &times; 29.7 cm, which is 8.27 &times; 11.69 in: narrower and taller than US Letter at
      8.5 &times; 11 in. For this reason, PDFs made for one paper size print with odd margins on the
      other. Airline cabin bag limits of 55 &times; 40 &times; 20 cm are 21.7 &times; 15.7 &times; 7.9
      in.
    </p>

    <h2>Heights and the feet-and-inches readout</h2>
    <p>
      For a human height, decimal inches are not useful. 175 cm is 68.9 in, which nobody says; it is
      5 ft 8.9 in, or "five foot nine". The feet-and-inches readout does that split and carries the
      remainder correctly, so you never get "5 ft 12 in" from simple rounding.
    </p>

    <h2>Converting a whole column</h2>
    <p>
      The Batch tab takes a pasted spreadsheet column and returns a matching one. Blank lines stay, so
      the output pastes back in the same rows. A line that is not a number stays blank and the tool
      does not guess, because a wrong number in a parts list is worse than a visible gap. The
      conversion runs in this tab, and nothing you paste is sent.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
